+        <article id="journal-text" class="journal-article">
+          <p>
+            Every single bag of coffee roasted in our facility begins months before green beans ever touch our cast-iron drum. It starts on remote, windswept mountain slopes with dirt roads, morning mists, and the shared passion of multi-generational coffee-farming families. For us, sourcing is neither a commercial transaction nor an automated commodity trade; it is an enduring covenant built on terroir, transparency, and human craftsmanship.
+          </p>
+
+          <h3>1. High Altitudes and Volcanic Terroir (1,800m+)</h3>
+          <p>
+            Coffee trees are remarkably responsive to microclimates. We source exclusively from high-altitude micro-lots located between 1,800 and 2,200 meters above sea level across the high Andes of Huila, Colombia, the mist-shrouded valleys of Yirgacheffe, Ethiopia, and the volcanic highlands of Antigua, Guatemala. At these lofty elevations, cool mountain nights dramatically slow the maturation of the coffee cherry. This extended gestation cycle allows the seed to pack itself with dense organic sugars, nuanced malic and phosphoric acids, and aromatic complexity. The jasmine and bergamot floral notes of our Ethiopian roasts and the crisp green-apple acidity with panela sweetness of our Colombian lots cannot be engineered inside a roastery—they must be coaxed from rich volcanic soil.
+          </p>
+
+          <h3>2. Selective Hand-Harvesting and Artisan Fermentation</h3>
+          <p>
+            Industrial coffee production relies on mechanical strip-harvesting that rips underripe, defective green fruit alongside ripe cherries. Our farming partners reject this compromise, relying on rigorous selective hand-picking. Highly trained harvesters move through the canopy in three to four separate passes throughout the harvest window, collecting only deep-crimson cherries at peak brix (sugar concentration). Once picked, the cherries undergo meticulous processing: either pristine washed fermentations using glacial mountain springs, or extended natural anaerobic fermentations where cherries dry in their own fruit on raised African beds, turned by hand every thirty minutes under the sun to maintain airflow and prevent spoilage.
+          </p>
+
+          <h3>3. Cupping Table Standards & The 86-Point Threshold</h3>
+          <p>
+            Before we contract any harvest, green bean samples are air-freighted to our cupping lab for blind sensory evaluation following Specialty Coffee Association (SCA) protocol. We test moisture retention, water activity, green density, and conduct multiple rounds of blind cuppings scoring fragrance, aroma, sweetness, clean cup, body, and acidity. While commercial specialty coffee accepts an 80-point grade, our threshold is uncompromising: every micro-lot must achieve an 86-point score or higher. Out of dozens of cupped samples each season, only the top three percent make it to our production roaster.
+          </p>
+
+          <h3>4. Direct-Trade Transparency & Regenerative Stewardship</h3>
+          <p>
+            True quality cannot exist without ethical prosperity. We trade directly with growers and their local cooperatives, cutting out predatory middlemen and export cartels. We pay an average of 40% above Fair Trade minimum prices directly to our producer partners, providing predictable income that insulates them from global commodity market speculation. These premiums directly finance clean water filtration facilities in local villages, provide scholarships for youth agronomy training, and sponsor shade-grown reforestation projects that restore native bird canopies and eliminate chemical fertilizers in favor of regenerative composting.
+          </p>
